    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Collagen for hair is supportive, not corrective.</strong> Hair fibre is keratin,
        and collagen supplies the amino acids keratin is built from, with glycine making up roughly
        one in three residues of the collagen molecule. The evidence here is much thinner than the
        evidence for skin, and honest expectations matter.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Mechanism, not magic.</strong> Collagen peptides feed the proline and glycine pool your follicles draw on and support the connective tissue around the dermal papilla. They do not become hair.</li>
        <li><strong>Best case is a protein-poor diet.</strong> If your intake is low, which is common on a vegetarian Indian diet, adding 8g of collagen protein a day plausibly helps shedding that was driven by that shortfall.</li>
        <li><strong>Give it 3 to 6 months.</strong> Hair grows about 1cm a month and the shedding you see today started 2 to 3 months ago. Nothing you swallow shows up faster than the cycle allows.</li>
        <li><strong>It will not treat pattern hair loss.</strong> Androgenetic alopecia is hormonal and needs medical treatment such as topical minoxidil or an oral prescription from a dermatologist. No collagen supplement changes that, and any brand telling you otherwise is lying.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

