      <h2 id="is-hair-collagen">Is hair made of collagen?</h2>

      <p>
        No. The hair shaft you can see and touch is made almost entirely of keratin, a hard fibrous
        protein that is chemically different from collagen. Collagen is the protein that forms the
        scaffolding of your skin, including the scalp skin your follicles sit inside, so its role in
        hair is indirect from the start.
      </p>

      <p>
        That distinction is where most collagen hair marketing quietly goes wrong. Nothing you
        swallow is threaded into a hair fibre in its original form. Proteins are dismantled in the
        gut into amino acids and short peptides, and the follicle then builds keratin from whatever
        amino acid pool is circulating.
      </p>

      <p>
        Where collagen becomes relevant is one level down. A hair follicle is a living organ that sits
        in the dermis, wrapped in a connective tissue sheath that is itself made of collagen, and fed
        by a small cluster of specialised cells at its base called the <strong>dermal papilla</strong>.
        The dermal papilla controls the growth cycle: how long a follicle produces fibre, when it
        rests, and how thick the shaft it makes is. That structure is dermal tissue, and dermal tissue
        is collagen-dependent.
      </p>

      <p>
        Collagen also happens to be unusually rich in the amino acids keratin construction leans on.
        Glycine accounts for roughly a third of every collagen molecule, and proline and
        hydroxyproline account for a large share of the rest. Human hair keratin is comparatively
        rich in cysteine, which collagen does not supply, so the overlap is partial rather than
        complete. Anyone who tells you collagen is "the building block of hair" has skipped that
        sentence.
      </p>

