      <h2 id="does-collagen-help-hair-growth">Does collagen help hair growth?</h2>

      <p>
        Possibly, in specific situations, and the effect is modest. Collagen peptides can support
        hair indirectly by topping up the amino acid pool follicles draw on and by supporting the
        dermal environment the follicle lives in. They do not stimulate new follicles, and they do
        not override a hormonal cause.
      </p>

      <p>
        There are two plausible routes, and it is worth separating them because they behave
        differently.
      </p>

      <p>
        The first is straightforwardly nutritional. Hair is metabolically expensive and it is also
        expendable, so the body deprioritises it fast when protein or micronutrients run short. A
        30ml shot delivering 8g of hydrolysed collagen protein is a meaningful addition for someone
        whose daily protein intake is genuinely low. In my OPD that describes a large share of the
        women who come in about shedding: two rotis, a katori of dal, some curd, and nothing else
        resembling protein in the day.
      </p>

      <p>
        The second route is the interesting one and also the more speculative one. Hydrolysed
        collagen is not just a protein source. Iwai and colleagues showed in 2005 that specific
        peptides, Pro-Hyp and Hyp-Gly, appear intact in human blood after ingestion, and these appear
        to act as signals to fibroblasts rather than as raw material. If they reach the perifollicular
        dermis, they may support the connective tissue environment around the follicle. I want to be
        clear that this is mechanistic reasoning, not proof of a hair outcome. We have measured the
        peptides in blood. We have not measured hair count improving because of them in a large
        trial. If you want the version of this science where the outcome data is much stronger, read
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-skin">what the evidence shows for skin</a>, where nineteen
        controlled trials exist and the endpoints were measured properly.
      </p>

