      <h2 id="evidence">What the evidence actually shows</h2>

      <p>
        The honest summary: there is no large, independent randomised controlled trial showing that
        hydrolysed collagen increases hair count or hair thickness in healthy adults. The evidence
        base for collagen and hair is thinner, smaller and more commercially entangled than the
        evidence base for collagen and skin, and any article that presents them as equally settled
        is misleading you.
      </p>

      <p>What does exist, and what it is worth:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Marine protein complex trials.</strong> Ablon published a three-month randomised,
          double-blind, placebo-controlled study of an extra-strength marine protein supplement in
          women with self-perceived thinning, reporting increases in terminal hair count. Two
          caveats matter. The product was a marine protein and micronutrient complex, not plain
          hydrolysed collagen, and the work was industry-supported. It is suggestive of a protein
          route, not proof that collagen specifically did the work.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Nutritional deficiency literature.</strong> This is the strongest and least
          glamorous evidence. Low ferritin, low protein intake, zinc deficiency and rapid weight
          loss are all documented causes of diffuse shedding, and correcting them improves hair.
          Collagen sits in this category as one contributor to protein intake.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Skin trial data, borrowed carefully.</strong> The 2021 meta-analysis in the
          <em>International Journal of Dermatology</em>, pooling 19 trials and 1,125 participants,
          established that hydrolysed collagen changes dermal parameters. Scalp is skin, so it is
          reasonable to expect the scalp dermis behaves similarly. Reasonable expectation is not
          measured hair outcome, and I would rather say that plainly than pretend the borrowing is
          free.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What is missing.</strong> Nobody has run a well-powered, independently funded trial
          of hydrolysed collagen alone with phototrichogram hair counts as the primary endpoint over
          six months. Until that exists, the correct posture is interested but unconvinced.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        I sell a collagen shot and I am telling you the hair evidence is weaker than the skin
        evidence. That is deliberate. If you buy Collabloom expecting it to regrow a receding
        hairline, you will be disappointed in four months and you will be right to be.
      </p>

      <h2 id="who-it-helps">Who collagen for hair helps, and who it does not</h2>

      <p>
        Collagen is most likely to help diffuse shedding that has a nutritional or recovery component
        behind it. It is least likely to help hair loss driven by hormones or by a specific deficiency
        that needs its own correction. Matching the cause to the intervention is the whole game, and
        it is the step most people skip.
      </p>

