      <h2 id="biotin-zinc">Biotin and zinc: the co-factors everyone asks about</h2>

      <p>
        Biotin and zinc genuinely matter for hair, but only in a specific way: supplementing them
        corrects a deficiency, it does not add benefit on top of sufficiency. Biotin is a B vitamin
        involved in keratin production, and zinc activates enzymes involved in protein synthesis and
        follicle function. If your levels are normal, more of either does very little.
      </p>

      <p>
        The review worth knowing is Patel and colleagues in <em>Skin Appendage Disorders</em>, 2017,
        which went through the published cases of biotin used for hair and nail problems. Every case
        with a clear benefit had an underlying cause of biotin deficiency, such as an inherited
        enzyme disorder, malabsorption or long-term antiepileptic use. The authors found no good
        evidence that biotin helps hair in people who are not deficient. Frank biotin deficiency is
        uncommon in people eating a normal mixed diet.
      </p>

      <p>
        So here is the inconvenient part about my own product. Collabloom contains
        <strong>45mcg of biotin and 1.5mg of zinc</strong>. Both are co-factor amounts included to
        support the formula, and both sit well below the doses used in the high-dose biotin case
        literature and below the zinc RDA of roughly 8 to 11mg a day. If you have a diagnosed zinc
        deficiency or a medical reason for high-dose biotin, a collagen shot is not your treatment
        and you should be taking a targeted supplement your doctor prescribes.
      </p>

      <p>
        There is a practical footnote on biotin that dermatologists repeat constantly and patients
        rarely hear. High-dose biotin, at the several-milligram level sold in many hair gummies, can
        interfere with laboratory immunoassays and produce false thyroid and cardiac troponin
        results. Stop those supplements for a few days before blood tests and tell the lab you take
        them. At 45mcg this is not a concern, but at the doses in a typical hair gummy it can be.
      </p>

      <p>
        The other co-factor question I get is vitamin C, and that one is simpler. Collagen synthesis
        in your own tissue is enzymatic and vitamin C is required for it, which is why a collagen
        formula without vitamin C is working against itself. Collabloom includes 250mg of vitamin C
        from amla. If you would rather get the same support from food and habits, we wrote a whole
        piece on <a href="/pages/boost-collagen-naturally">boosting collagen naturally</a>.
      </p>

