      <h2 id="timeline">How long does collagen take to work for hair?</h2>

      <p>
        Plan on three to six months before you judge it, and six months before you decide it failed.
        Scalp hair grows at roughly 1cm a month, so any new fibre produced under better nutritional
        conditions needs several months just to become long enough to see. Hair is the slowest tissue
        to reward anything you do for it.
      </p>

      <p>
        Understanding the cycle makes the wait less maddening. Each follicle runs through three
        phases. <strong>Anagen</strong> is active growth and lasts two to six years, which is why
        around 90% of your follicles are in it at any given moment. <strong>Catagen</strong> is a
        short transition of a couple of weeks. <strong>Telogen</strong> is a resting phase of about
        three months, at the end of which the old fibre is released and a new one starts pushing
        through.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is why sudden shedding always confuses people about cause. A stressor pushes an
        abnormally large number of follicles out of anagen and into telogen at once, but the hair does
        not fall for another two to three months. Kligman described this lag in 1961 and it still
        catches everyone out. The dengue you had in June is the shedding you panic about in
        September. It also means anything you start today is working against a queue of hairs that
        were already committed to falling before you began.
      </p>

      <p>A realistic month-by-month expectation:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Weeks 1 to 4.</strong> Nothing visible in hair. If you notice anything, it is skin hydration, which moves faster than any follicle can.</li>
        <li><strong>Weeks 4 to 8.</strong> Shedding may still look unchanged or briefly worse if you are mid-effluvium. This is the queue emptying, not the supplement failing.</li>
        <li><strong>Months 3 to 4.</strong> The first honest signal. Fewer hairs on the pillow, short new growth along the hairline, nails splitting less.</li>
        <li><strong>Months 4 to 6.</strong> Density and shaft quality, if they are going to change, change here. Take a photo in the same light on day one so you have something to compare against, because you will not trust your memory.</li>
        <li><strong>Beyond 6 months.</strong> If nothing has moved, stop and get investigated. Ferritin, thyroid profile, vitamin D and a scalp examination. Continuing a supplement that has not worked for half a year is money spent avoiding a diagnosis.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="choosing">What is the best collagen for hair growth?</h2>

      <p>
        The best collagen for hair growth is the one with a real dose printed on the box, delivered in
        a form you will take every day for six months. Type I collagen from a marine source is the
        sensible default because it is the dominant collagen of skin, and scalp is skin. Everything
        after that is execution.
      </p>

      <p>Four checks, in the order I would apply them:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Is the dose in milligrams?</strong> Positive collagen trials in skin used 2.5g to
          10g a day. If a hair gummy lists "collagen blend" with no number, assume the number is
          small enough to be decorative.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Is it hydrolysed?</strong> Whole collagen is around 300,000 Daltons and behaves
          like ordinary dietary protein. Hydrolysed peptides under roughly 2,000 Daltons are what the
          absorption research is about. More on that in
          <a href="/pages/collagen-peptides">how collagen peptides work</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>How much sugar comes with it?</strong> Many collagen drinks and almost all gummies
          carry 8 to 12g of sugar per serving. Dietary sugar drives glycation, which stiffens the
          collagen you already have. Collabloom is 35 kcal with zero sugar.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Will you actually take it daily?</strong> Adherence beats formulation. A 30ml shot
          you drink in four seconds gets taken more reliably than a powder you have to blend, and at
          a typical Indian retail price around 1,899 rupees a pack, an untouched box is the most
          expensive option available.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        One thing I want to close on, because it is the sentence patients most need and least want.
        If your parting is widening or your temples are receding, you have a medical condition with
        proper treatments, and a collagen shot is not one of them. See a dermatologist. Get the
        ferritin test. Start minoxidil if it is indicated. Then, if you want nutritional support
        alongside proper treatment, add collagen. In that order, and not the other way round.
      </p>

