      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen really help hair growth?</summary>
          <p>
            It can support hair indirectly, but it does not stimulate growth the way a medical
            treatment does. Collagen supplies glycine and proline for keratin synthesis and supports
            the dermal tissue around the follicle, which matters most if your protein intake is low.
            There is no large independent trial showing hydrolysed collagen increases hair count in
            healthy adults, so keep expectations modest.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How long does collagen take to work for hair?</summary>
          <p>
            Allow three to six months. Scalp hair grows about 1cm a month, and shedding you see today
            was triggered two to three months ago, so nothing you swallow shows results faster than
            the hair cycle permits. If nothing has changed by six months, get your ferritin, thyroid
            and vitamin D checked instead of continuing.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is collagen or biotin better for hair?</summary>
          <p>
            Neither is a treatment, and biotin only helps people who are actually deficient. A 2017
            review in Skin Appendage Disorders found benefit only in cases with an underlying cause
            of biotin deficiency, and no good evidence in people with normal levels. Collagen has a
            broader nutritional rationale because it contributes 8g of protein, but the hair evidence
            for it is also limited.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can collagen stop hair fall from pattern baldness?</summary>
          <p>
            No. Androgenetic alopecia is driven by hormonal sensitivity of the follicle and needs
            medical treatment, typically topical minoxidil and in some cases an oral prescription from
            a dermatologist. Using a supplement instead of treatment costs you the years in which
            treatment works best.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Will collagen help post-partum hair fall?</summary>
          <p>
            It can support the recovery but it does not cause it. Post-partum shedding is telogen
            effluvium triggered by the hormonal shift after delivery, and it usually resolves on its
            own between 6 and 12 months. Adequate protein, iron and overall nutrition help the regrowth
            phase, so speak to your doctor about ferritin before adding anything.
          </p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much collagen should I take per day for hair?</summary>
          <p>
            There is no dose established specifically for hair, so the sensible approach is to use the
            range studied for skin, which is 2.5g to 10g of hydrolysed collagen peptides daily.
            Collabloom delivers 8,000mg per 30ml shot. Doses below 2.5g have almost no supporting
            evidence for any outcome.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>

